import "core"


# DISABLED — a compiler gap, kept as the repro of it.
  The default arrow. A name with no arguments applied is a value in its own right — the partial
  application `f` has before anything is given to it — so an arrow may be written from it.
  `f= f(b= 2, c= 3)` rewrites that bare `f`, and the `a` of `f(a= 1)` is applied on top of what
  the rewrite produced, reaching the full `f(a, b, c)`. So `b` and `c` are defaults, supplied
  when the call site does not. The defaults are distinct and non-zero so the answer would
  distinguish a default that landed in the wrong slot.
  Two gaps were in the way. The first is fixed and is what `partialApply.ct` now holds: an
  application's base was never rewritten through its own arrows, so the argument landed on an
  empty tuple and the arrow's arguments were lost (`g(a= 1)` evaluating to `/f(/a=1)`, with no
  `/b` or `/c` for `f`'s definition to read). The base meta holds the arrow's result BESIDE the
  raw name (`ACT (/g + /f(/b=/N))` — the raw form is kept for eval's dispatch keying), so the
  base is a subtype of its own destination and `buildCallTree` short-circuits to `TCTId`.
  `TreeBuild.baseBuildMeta` builds it toward what it settles to instead.
  The second gap is why this file is disabled, and it is only reached when the name is the SAME
  on both sides — `partialApply.ct` writes the same thing under two names and passes.
  A bare-name arrow's body is built FROM the name it rewrites, so the body's base reaches the
  arrow being defined and the arrow's result is defined in terms of itself. That does not
  converge from above: the base meta unions the raw name with the arrow's own still-unresolved
  TopType result, TopType absorbs the raw form, `addArgToType` has nothing to graft the body's
  arguments onto, and the result never leaves TopType — so the definition and every call of it
  stay TopType for the whole solve. Reduced, with no import of core:
    data(N)
    f(a -> N, b -> N) -> N= a
    f= f(b= N)
    main= f(a= N)
  types `f= f(b= N)` at TopType and `f(a= N)` at TopType, where the same program under two names
  types them `/f(/b=/N)` and `/f(/a=/N, /b=/N)`.
  `TypeCheck.TypeUtils.dropSelfBaseReach` is the ruling for that — an arrow does not rewrite the
  base of its own body, which is what TreeBuild already refuses outright ("Found cyclical use of
  function", `arrowFollowup`). It fixes the reduced program above: the arrow's result seeds from
  the raw name and settles to `/f(/b=/N)` in three epochs, and `f(a= N)` becomes
  `(/f(/a=/N, /b=/N) + /f(/a=/N))`.
  What is left is the cost of that second leaf at the scale of core. `f(a= 1)` keeps the raw
  `/f(/a=1)` beside the settled `/f(/a=1, /b=2, /c=3)`, and the raw one cannot be settled by the
  reach machinery: nothing rewrites `/f(/a)` itself, because its rewrite runs through re-rewriting
  its BASE, which a query for the arrows accepting `/f(/a)` cannot see. So `dropSettleForcedRaws`
  keeps it (`reachesTo` finds no path), and it rides into everything downstream. With core
  imported this file goes from about 30 seconds to over 40 minutes with no sign of finishing, at a
  flat ~250MB — slow, not a leak. It is not the equality dispatch on the result: the same file
  written as `main{io -> IO}= io.exit(val= f(a= 0))`, with no `==` anywhere, times out the same
  way. Without core the reduced program above solves in under a second, so it is the interaction
  of the unsettleable raw leaf with a large program, not the arrow itself.
f(a -> Integer, b -> Integer, c -> Integer) -> Integer= a + b + c
f= f(b= 2, c= 3)

main{io -> IO} =
  #assert(test= f(a= 1) == 6)
  io
